#!/usr/bin/env bash
# cc-spawn-verify — did the session I just spawned actually START?
#
# THE GAP THIS CLOSES. A spawn is fire-and-forget: the Agent tool returns "Spawned successfully"
# the moment the pane is requested, not when the agent is running. Everything between those two
# facts is invisible to the caller, and the failures there are SILENT — the pane exists, so nothing
# looks broken, and the lead waits forever on an agent that never started. Measured 2026-08-03: two
# of four subagents in one wave died at the shell (`zsh: command not found: acd`, and the same
# corruption escalated by `setopt CORRECT` into an interactive `correct 'acd' to 'cd' [nyae]?`
# prompt that hangs indefinitely). Both panes stayed alive and idle. Nothing reported anything.
#
# 0999c8bb closes the dominant CAUSE of that (kitty was stealing focus mid-launch, so operator
# keystrokes landed in the launch command line). This file closes the DETECTION half, which is
# disjoint: a spawn can still fail to start for reasons that have nothing to do with typing — a
# logged-out account, a usage-limit refusal at first token, a reaped cwd, a binary that moved.
#
# FIVE OUTCOMES, NOT TWO — and everything after the second is the whole point (same contract as
# handoff-fire.sh:verify_engagement, deliberately, so consumers learn ONE exit-code vocabulary):
#
#   0  RUNNING  — the agent process exists AND is not sitting on a modal. It started, and it is
#                 free to proceed.
#   1  ABSENT   — no such process, and no evidence of why. Died, or never launched.
#   2  PARKED   — no such process AND a pane is sitting at a shell prompt showing a launch that
#                 died. This is knowable in SECONDS and names its own remedy; folding it into 1
#                 loses the diagnosis. Callers MUST branch on 2 explicitly.
#   3  OFFBOX   — the subject is a DECLARED off-box (cloud) session, so this box's process table is
#                 structurally unable to answer the question. A NON-VERDICT, not a severity.
#   4  WEDGED   — the process EXISTS and is inert on a blocking modal. The exact inverse of 2, and
#                 the state this file used to report as 0 RUNNING — see below.
#   5  UNPROVEN — RESERVED, and NOT PRODUCED BY THIS FILE. handoff-fire.sh:verify_engagement spends
#                 it for "the scan could not answer" (brief ingested, no assistant turn yet; or the
#                 scan itself errored). It is listed here because this vocabulary is shared and a
#                 number may not carry two meanings — the same reason that file spends 5 rather than
#                 reusing the 3 already taken above. A caller of BOTH must therefore treat 5 as a
#                 NON-VERDICT (like 3), never as a failure: it says the question went unanswered, not
#                 that the answer was no, so it must not trigger a re-fire.
#
# 5 REACHED THIS FILE LATE, AND THAT IS THE BUG THIS ENTRY ALSO RECORDS (backlog eece3244fca5).
# `6509abd2` (2026-08-11) added `return 5` to verify_engagement as a LOCAL edit — its own contract
# header, its signature and its call-site comment all still said FOUR, and this file, the declared
# second consumer, was never told the member existed. The paragraph below states the rule it broke:
# a new member of a shared exit vocabulary "arrives here WITH its second consumer taught, never as a
# local edit". An auditor reading either file's prose would have concluded 5 did not exist.
#
# ── WHY 4 EXISTS: LIVENESS IS NOT PROGRESS, AND 0 WAS ANSWERING THE WRONG QUESTION (plan §9.5) ───
# A fresh session in a project whose `.mcp.json` declares an unapproved server renders "New MCP
# server found in this project: …" and BLOCKS. That is a live `claude.exe` doing no work: `ps` says
# healthy, `agent_pid` below finds it, and this file said `✓ RUNNING`. Every state 1/2/3 models an
# agent that is NOT in the process table; 4 is the one where it is, which is why no amount of
# sharpening the process oracle could ever have reached it. The plan filed it as a follow-on rather
# than forcing it through with the tool's own author absent, precisely because a new member of a
# shared exit vocabulary lands in every consumer's `*)` arm (memory:
# new-enum-member-falls-into-fail-closed-default) — so it arrives here WITH its second consumer
# taught, never as a local edit.
#
# The oracle is `hooks/lib/pane-modal.sh`, shared verbatim with handoff-fire.sh so the enumerated
# dialog strings exist in exactly ONE place; read its header before touching a pattern. It fails
# OPEN in every direction — no kitty, no socket, no python3, no pane found, an unreadable screen, a
# missing lib — and an open failure yields 0 RUNNING, i.e. exactly the answer this file gave before
# the state existed. A verdict this file cannot reach is never worse than the one it used to give.
#
# ── THE FOLD RANK IS EXPLICIT, AND IS NOT THE NUMERIC ORDER ──────────────────────────────────────
# `--all` reports the worst member. That used to be `max(rc)`, which silently made "highest number"
# mean "worst" — so the ordering principle lived nowhere and the NEXT member would inherit the top
# rank by arithmetic accident. It is now a table, and the principle it encodes is stated once:
#
#     RUNNING  <  ABSENT  <  PARKED  <  WEDGED  <  OFFBOX
#        0            1         2         4          3
#
# ABSENT < PARKED < WEDGED orders by SPECIFICITY of diagnosis, not by severity — WEDGED is the most
# specific of the three (it names a live pid, a named dialog, and a one-keystroke remedy) and it is
# the one a caller is most likely to mis-read as healthy, since it is literally the state that read
# as green until now. OFFBOX stays on top because it is the only NON-VERDICT in the set: a wave
# containing a question this box could not ask must not report as a wave whose members were all
# answered (memory: gate-never-ran-vs-gate-red). No existing input changes verdict — 4 did not
# exist, so nothing that used to fold to 3 can now fold to 4.
#
# 5 (UNPROVEN) HAS NO RANK IN THIS TABLE, deliberately: this file never produces it, so a rank here
# would be an untested claim about a member that cannot appear. Stated rather than left blank for
# exactly the reason this section exists — a member with no explicit rank is the one that inherits
# the top by arithmetic accident the moment someone reintroduces `max(rc)`. If a future change makes
# this file produce 5, it earns its rank in this table in the SAME diff, as a NON-VERDICT beside 3.
#
# ── WHY 3 EXISTS, AND WHY IT OUTRANKS THE OTHERS (CLOUD_OBSERVABILITY.md §5.2, liar #1) ──────────
# A session running in an Anthropic-managed VM shares no kernel and no process table with this box.
# `agent_pid` below scans the LOCAL process table, so for such a session it returns empty — every
# time, by construction — and this file then printed `✗ ABSENT … Died, or never launched.` That is
# not a missing feature, it is a FABRICATED DEATH VERDICT: the instrument converts "not on this box"
# into "dead", and a caller acting on it kills or re-fires a perfectly healthy session.
#
# So the absence path now asks `cc-cloud is-offbox <id>` FIRST — before the wait loop, because
# waiting `$TIMEOUT` seconds for a process that can never appear is burning the clock to reach a
# wrong answer — and abstains when the id is declared. It is checked before `parked_evidence` too:
# for a declared off-box id, LOCAL evidence of any kind is inadmissible, including a pane that
# happens to quote the launch.
#
# 3 outranks 2 in the worst-verdict fold below because it is a NON-VERDICT (memory:
# gate-never-ran-vs-gate-red — one exit code for "asked and got no" and "could not ask" forces every
# caller to lie). A wave containing one off-box member is NOT verified-green, and must not read as
# one; the caller is told exactly which member it could not answer for.
#
# Binding a local name to a cloud id: `--cloud <id>`. With no flag the NAME ITSELF is tried as an
# id, which is the common case (a cloud session is addressed by its `session_…` id, and there is no
# local pane to give it another name). An UNDECLARED id is not off-box — `is-offbox` returns 1 and
# the ordinary local verdict stands, so this can only ever ABSTAIN, never manufacture a green.
#
# WHY PROCESS-FIRST, NOT PANE-FIRST. The process table is the authority on "is it running": the
# harness stamps `--agent-name <n>` into the child's argv, so the agent is directly addressable and
# a match is proof. Pane state is only consulted to EXPLAIN an absence, never to establish presence
# — a pane oracle can be blind (kitty not running, remote control off, iTerm2 instead) and a blind
# oracle must never be able to convict a healthy agent.
#
# WHY NOT `foreground_processes`. It is the foreground process GROUP, sampled: on this fleet live
# claude panes variously showed `caffeinate`, `tee`, `chrome-devtools-mcp` and an MCP server
# alongside the binary. It over-reports rather than under-reports (29/29 live panes did contain
# `claude`), so it is not WRONG — but it is a sample with no stability guarantee, and it is the
# wrong thing to key on when the process table answers the same question exactly.
# `in_alternate_screen` is a terminal-STATE fact (the TUI switched buffers), not a process sample,
# and it separates the states we care about cleanly — measured across 29 live panes plus a bare-zsh
# control plus a replay of the `[nyae]` hang:
#     running a TUI : in_alternate_screen=True   at_prompt=False
#     bare prompt   : in_alternate_screen=False  at_prompt=True
#     [nyae] hang   : in_alternate_screen=False  at_prompt=True
# It answers "is a full-screen TUI running", not "is claude running" — vim would read True too.
# That is sufficient here because it is only ever used to explain an ALREADY-ESTABLISHED absence.
#
# Usage:  cc-spawn-verify <agent-name> [--timeout N] [--quiet] [--cloud <session-id>]
#         cc-spawn-verify --all <name>...      → verifies each, exits non-zero if ANY is not RUNNING
set -euo pipefail

TIMEOUT="${CC_SPAWN_VERIFY_TIMEOUT:-25}"
INTERVAL="${CC_SPAWN_VERIFY_INTERVAL:-1}"
QUIET=0
ALL=0
CLOUD_ID=""
NAMES=()

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --cloud) CLOUD_ID="${2:-}"; shift 2 ;;
    --timeout) TIMEOUT="${2:-25}"; shift 2 ;;
    --interval) INTERVAL="${2:-1}"; shift 2 ;;
    --quiet|-q) QUIET=1; shift ;;
    --all) ALL=1; shift ;;
    # Bounded by the SHEBANG-to-`set` span, never a line count. `2,40p` was correct when the header
    # was 40 lines and silently truncated mid-sentence the moment a state was added to it — a help
    # window whose correctness depends on nobody editing the file above it.
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,/^set -euo pipefail$/p' "$0" | sed '$d'; exit 0 ;;
    -*) printf 'cc-spawn-verify: unknown option %s\n' "$1" >&2; exit 64 ;;
    *) NAMES+=("$1"); shift ;;
  esac
done
[ ${#NAMES[@]} -gt 0 ] || { printf 'usage: cc-spawn-verify <agent-name> [--timeout N] [--quiet]\n' >&2; exit 64; }

say() { [ "$QUIET" = 1 ] || printf '%s\n' "$*"; }

# ── THE ABSTAIN LOOKUP (see "WHY 3 EXISTS" in the header) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Resolve cc-cloud PATH-first so a test shim wins, then the standard install path, then this repo's
# own bin/ (this file is run straight out of a worktree during development). Seam: CC_CLOUD_BIN —
# SET, including set to EMPTY, is honored verbatim, so `CC_CLOUD_BIN=` genuinely disables the
# lookup. `${VAR:-}` cannot tell unset from set-empty, and a seam that cannot turn a thing OFF is
# not a seam (same contract as team-orphan-reaper.sh's TEAM_REAPER_LSOF_BIN).
if [ -n "${CC_CLOUD_BIN+set}" ]; then
  CC_CLOUD="$CC_CLOUD_BIN"
else
  CC_CLOUD="$(command -v cc-cloud 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ -n "$CC_CLOUD" ] || { [ -x "$HOME/.claude/bin/cc-cloud" ] && CC_CLOUD="$HOME/.claude/bin/cc-cloud"; }
  [ -n "$CC_CLOUD" ] || { [ -x "$(dirname "$0")/cc-cloud" ] && CC_CLOUD="$(dirname "$0")/cc-cloud"; }
fi

# ── THE MODAL ENUMERATION (see "WHY 4 EXISTS") ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared verbatim with scripts/handoff-fire.sh. Resolution order mirrors the cc-cloud block above,
# with the repo-relative path FIRST rather than last: this file is a per-file symlink into the
# checkout, so a lib added in the same commit is present beside it immediately, while the installed
# ~/.claude/hooks/lib/ copy only appears on the next install.sh glob (bin/cc-notify:199 names the
# same deploy-lag trap). Absent lib ⇒ no wedge oracle ⇒ 0 RUNNING, exactly the pre-4 behaviour.
#
# `if` blocks, never `[ … ] && …` — this file is `set -e`, and its own fold comment already names
# that shape as the one that quietly inverts a guard. A source-guard that aborted the script when
# the lib was ABSENT would turn the fail-open design into a fail-dead one.
#
# CC_PANE_MODAL_LIB is the same `+set` seam handoff-fire.sh uses for this lib, and it is what lets a
# test exercise the ABSENT-lib path: `CC_PANE_MODAL_LIB=` disables the oracle outright. Without an
# off switch the repo-relative candidate always resolves and the fail-open branch would be untested
# — i.e. the branch that decides whether a missing deploy degrades or breaks.
CC_MODAL_LIB=""
if [ -n "${CC_PANE_MODAL_LIB+set}" ]; then
  if [ -f "${CC_PANE_MODAL_LIB}" ]; then CC_MODAL_LIB="$CC_PANE_MODAL_LIB"; fi
else
  for _c in "$(dirname "$0")/../hooks/lib/pane-modal.sh" \
            "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/hooks/lib/pane-modal.sh" \
            "$HOME/.claude/hooks/lib/pane-modal.sh"; do
    if [ -f "$_c" ]; then CC_MODAL_LIB="$_c"; break; fi
  done
fi
if [ -n "$CC_MODAL_LIB" ]; then
  # shellcheck source=../hooks/lib/pane-modal.sh
  # shellcheck disable=SC1091  # runtime-resolved; the ship gate runs shellcheck without -x
  . "$CC_MODAL_LIB"
fi

# ── THE KITTY LOOKUP — ONE resolver, TWO pane oracles ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hoisted out of parked_evidence when the wedge oracle arrived, so this file does not carry two
# spellings of the same RPC (plan §8.3's standing "the predicate is now in THREE files" risk, in
# miniature). Resolution is byte-identical to what parked_evidence shipped with — deliberately NOT
# widened to bin/cc-kitty-bin's absolute-path candidates, which would make a previously fail-open
# caller start finding kitty and is a behaviour change this item did not ask for.
#
# CC_KITTY_BIN uses the `+set` seam, so `CC_KITTY_BIN=` genuinely disables both oracles. That is
# what makes a test hermetic against the operator's live fleet: PATH-prepending a stub directory
# does NOT remove the real `kitten` further down PATH, so before this seam existed a suite could
# reach 20 live panes while its own comment claimed there was no kitty.
if [ -n "${CC_KITTY_BIN+set}" ]; then
  KITTY_BIN="$CC_KITTY_BIN"
else
  KITTY_BIN="$(command -v kitten 2>/dev/null || command -v kitty 2>/dev/null || true)"
fi

kitty_ls() { # → the window tree as JSON on stdout, empty if kitty cannot answer
  local sock
  [ -n "$KITTY_BIN" ] || return 0
  sock="${CC_TERM_KITTY_TO:-${KITTY_LISTEN_ON:-}}"
  if [ -n "$sock" ]; then "$KITTY_BIN" @ --to "$sock" ls 2>/dev/null || true
  else "$KITTY_BIN" @ ls 2>/dev/null || true; fi
}

kitty_text() { # $1=pane-id → that pane's PLAIN visible text (no --ansi; see pane-modal.sh's contract)
  local sock
  [ -n "$KITTY_BIN" ] || return 0
  sock="${CC_TERM_KITTY_TO:-${KITTY_LISTEN_ON:-}}"
  if [ -n "$sock" ]; then "$KITTY_BIN" @ --to "$sock" get-text --match "id:$1" 2>/dev/null || true
  else "$KITTY_BIN" @ get-text --match "id:$1" 2>/dev/null || true; fi
}

# is_offbox <name> → 0 iff the subject is a DECLARED, unretired cloud session.
# Fails CLOSED toward the ordinary local verdict: no cc-cloud, an unreadable state dir, or an
# undeclared id all return 1, which leaves ABSENT/PARKED exactly as they were. This can only ever
# suppress a false death, never invent a false life.
is_offbox() {
  local id="${CLOUD_ID:-$1}"
  [ -n "$id" ] || return 1
  [ -n "$CC_CLOUD" ] && [ -x "$CC_CLOUD" ] || return 1
  "$CC_CLOUD" is-offbox "$id" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# ── IS THE AGENT PROCESS RUNNING? ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TWO conditions, and BOTH are load-bearing — this took a false RUNNING to get right.
#
#   1. `--agent-name <n>` as an ARGV PAIR, never the bare name anywhere in the line. A plain
#      `pgrep -f <n>` counts every process whose argv merely MENTIONS the name, and briefs are
#      passed as argv, so a lead that wrote the name into a brief matches ITSELF.
#   2. The EXECUTABLE (argv[0], the field right after the pid) must be a claude binary.
#
# Condition 1 alone is NOT enough, which is the counter-intuitive part: the pair `--agent-name <n>`
# also appears verbatim in every command line that merely QUOTES the launch — the spawning shell,
# a `kitten @ send-text` carrying the launch text, the operator's own grep. Measured while testing
# this file: a pane was visibly hung on `correct 'acd' to 'cd' [nyae]?` with no agent running, and
# this function reported `RUNNING (pid 54740)` — pid 54740 being the test harness whose own argv
# contained the flags. A verifier that can be satisfied by the sentence describing the spawn is
# worse than no verifier: it returns green in exactly the case it exists to catch.
#
# So the discriminator is argv POSITION, not text presence. The real agent EXECUTES claude; every
# impostor merely mentions it.
agent_pid() { # $1=name → echoes pid(s) of genuine agent processes, empty if none
  ps -Ao pid=,command= 2>/dev/null \
    | awk -v n="$1" '
        $2 !~ /claude/ { next }                 # argv[0] must BE claude, not quote it
        {
          for (i = 3; i < NF; i++)
            if ($i == "--agent-name" && $(i+1) == n) { print $1; break }
        }'
}

# ── WHY IS IT ABSENT? (diagnosis only — never used to prove presence) ────────────────────────────
# Fails OPEN in every direction: no kitty, no socket, unreadable JSON, python missing ⇒ "no
# evidence", i.e. exit 1. A diagnostic that could itself report PARKED on its own blindness would
# be worse than no diagnostic at all.
#
# SPECIFICITY IS THE WHOLE DESIGN HERE. "A pane is at a shell prompt" is NOT evidence of a dead
# spawn — the operator legitimately keeps shell panes open, so keying on that alone would report
# PARKED for every ABSENT and the verdict would carry zero bits. A pane only counts as evidence if
# its own screen NAMES THIS AGENT: the corrupted line still contains `--agent-name <n>`, because
# what died is the launch command itself. That makes the diagnosis specific to the spawn we asked
# about and immune to unrelated shells.
parked_evidence() { # $1=agent-name → echoes a reason and returns 0 if THIS agent's launch died
  local name="$1" out
  out="$(kitty_ls)"
  [ -n "$out" ] || return 1
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1

  # Candidate panes: at a shell prompt (not in the alternate screen). Narrowed below by content.
  local cands
  cands="$(printf '%s' "$out" | python3 -c '
import json, sys
try:
    d = json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception:
    sys.exit(0)
for w in d:
    for t in w.get("tabs", []):
        for win in t.get("windows", []):
            if win.get("in_alternate_screen") is False and win.get("at_prompt") is True:
                print(win.get("id"))
' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ -n "$cands" ] || return 1

  local id text found=""
  while IFS= read -r id; do
    [ -n "$id" ] || continue
    text="$(kitty_text "$id")"
    [ -n "$text" ] || continue
    # Must name THIS agent — the dead launch line still carries `--agent-name <n>`.
    printf '%s' "$text" | grep -F -- "--agent-name $name" >/dev/null || continue
    # Line-ANCHORED shell-error match. Unanchored would match our own prose: this very file quotes
    # `correct 'acd' to 'cd' [nyae]?`, and a pane showing this source would read as a wedge.
    # Both shells' orderings are carried — zsh says `zsh: command not found: X`, bash says
    # `bash: X: command not found`; one pattern silently covers only zsh.
    # `.*` not `[^\n]*` — grep is already line-oriented, and spelling the negated class out with a
    # literal newline inside the brackets makes grep read the pattern as two, which it rejects with
    # "brackets ([ ]) not balanced". That failure is SILENT here (stderr, `|| true`), and it
    # degraded the verdict from a named cause to a generic one while still exiting 2 — caught only
    # because the probe printed the real screen alongside the diagnosis.
    local why
    why="$(printf '%s' "$text" | grep -oE '^((zsh|bash): (correct |command not found|no such file or directory|no matches found).*|bash: [^:]*: (command not found|no such file or directory))' | tail -1 || true)"
    if [ -n "$why" ]; then found="pane id=$id — $why"; else found="pane id=$id — launch line present, never executed"; fi
    break
  done <<< "$cands"

  [ -n "$found" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$found"
  return 0
}

# ── IS THE RUNNING AGENT ACTUALLY FREE TO PROCEED? (the 4 WEDGED oracle) ─────────────────────────
# Runs ONLY once presence is already proven, so it can never establish presence — the file's
# process-first rule is unchanged, and this is a second consultation of pane state for the same
# reason parked_evidence is the first: to EXPLAIN a fact the process table has already settled.
#
# THE JOIN IS ARGV POSITION, NOT PID. `foreground_processes` is a sampled process GROUP, so the
# agent's pid is not guaranteed to be the one kitty reports for its window at the instant we look;
# joining on the pid would make the answer depend on that sample. Matching `--agent-name <n>` as an
# argv PAIR with a claude argv[0] is the SAME discriminator agent_pid uses, so the two oracles agree
# by construction rather than by coincidence, and the `pgrep -f matches briefs` failure that this
# file already paid for once cannot come back through a second door.
#
# FAILS OPEN, IN EVERY DIRECTION — no lib, no kitty, no python3, no matching window, a blank screen,
# an unenumerated dialog. Every one of those yields "not wedged" ⇒ 0 RUNNING ⇒ precisely the verdict
# this file gave before the state existed. A blind wedge oracle can never convict a healthy agent.
wedge_evidence() { # $1=agent-name → echoes "<slug> — pane id=<id>" and returns 0 if inert on a modal
  local name="$1" out ids id text slug
  command -v pane_modal_reason >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  out="$(kitty_ls)"
  [ -n "$out" ] || return 1
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1

  ids="$(printf '%s' "$out" | AGENT_NAME="$name" python3 -c '
import json, os, posixpath, sys
want = os.environ["AGENT_NAME"]
try:
    d = json.load(sys.stdin)
except Exception:
    sys.exit(0)
def is_agent(argv):
    # argv[0] must BE claude (a brief can contain any text; it cannot occupy argv[0]), and the flag
    # must be its own list element with the name as the NEXT one.
    if not argv or "claude" not in posixpath.basename(argv[0]):
        return False
    for i, a in enumerate(argv[:-1]):
        if a == "--agent-name" and argv[i + 1] == want:
            return True
    return False
for w in d:
    for t in w.get("tabs", []):
        for win in t.get("windows", []):
            for p in (win.get("foreground_processes") or []):
                if is_agent(p.get("cmdline") or []):
                    print(win.get("id"))
                    break
' 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ -n "$ids" ] || return 1

  while IFS= read -r id; do
    [ -n "$id" ] || continue
    text="$(kitty_text "$id")"
    [ -n "$text" ] || continue
    slug="$(printf '%s' "$text" | pane_modal_reason || true)"
    [ -n "$slug" ] || continue
    printf '%s — pane id=%s\n' "$slug" "$id"
    return 0
  done <<< "$ids"
  return 1
}

verify_one() { # $1=name → 0 running / 1 absent / 2 parked / 3 offbox / 4 wedged
  local name="$1" waited=0 pids wedge
  # FIRST, before the clock: a declared off-box session can never appear in the local process
  # table, so waiting for it is spending $TIMEOUT to arrive at a wrong answer.
  if is_offbox "$name"; then
    say "◇ OFFBOX   $name (${CLOUD_ID:-$name}) — declared off-box; this box's process table cannot"
    say "           see it. NOT a death verdict. Liveness: cc-cloud show ${CLOUD_ID:-$name}"
    return 3
  fi
  while :; do
    pids="$(agent_pid "$name" || true)"
    if [ -n "$pids" ]; then
      # Presence is settled; the remaining question is whether it can PROCEED. Checked here rather
      # than after the loop because the loop RETURNS on the first sighting — a check placed after it
      # would be unreachable on the only path that reaches it.
      if wedge="$(wedge_evidence "$name")"; then
        say "✗ WEDGED   $name (pid $(printf '%s' "$pids" | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//')) — the process"
        say "           exists and is INERT on a blocking dialog: $wedge"
        say "           $(pane_modal_remedy "${wedge%% *}")"
        say "           ps says healthy and no hook has fired, which is why nothing else reports it."
        return 4
      fi
      say "✓ RUNNING  $name (pid $(printf '%s' "$pids" | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//'))"
      return 0
    fi
    [ "$waited" -ge "$TIMEOUT" ] && break
    sleep "$INTERVAL"; waited=$((waited + INTERVAL))
  done
  local why
  if why="$(parked_evidence "$name")"; then
    say "✗ PARKED   $name — no agent process after ${TIMEOUT}s; a pane is sitting at a shell:"
    say "$(printf '%s' "$why" | sed 's/^/             /')"
    say "           the launch line died before claude started (corrupted command, bad cwd, or a"
    say "           launcher that exited). The pane is ALIVE, which is why nothing else reports it."
    return 2
  fi
  say "✗ ABSENT   $name — no agent process after ${TIMEOUT}s, and no parked pane to explain it."
  return 1
}

# Worst-verdict wins, so a wave is only green when EVERY member is running. The ORDER is the table
# in the header, and it is spelled out here rather than inferred from `max(rc)` — which is what this
# fold used to be, and which quietly meant "the highest exit code is the worst outcome". That held
# only by arithmetic luck up to 3, and the very next member (4 WEDGED) breaks it: WEDGED is a
# verdict and OFFBOX is a NON-VERDICT, so OFFBOX must stay on top no matter what number it wears
# (memory: new-enum-member-falls-into-fail-closed-default — a new member lands in every derivation's
# default arm, and `max()` IS a default arm).
#
# An unmapped code sorts ABOVE every mapped one but below OFFBOX's non-verdict: an outcome this
# table has not been taught is, by definition, one nobody has reasoned about — it must never fold
# down into green, and it must never impersonate the abstain.
rank_of() { # $1=exit code → its position in RUNNING < ABSENT < PARKED < WEDGED < OFFBOX
  case "$1" in
    0) printf 0 ;;   # RUNNING
    1) printf 1 ;;   # ABSENT
    2) printf 2 ;;   # PARKED
    4) printf 3 ;;   # WEDGED
    3) printf 5 ;;   # OFFBOX — the only NON-VERDICT, and therefore the top
    *) printf 4 ;;   # unmapped: louder than every verdict, quieter than the abstain
  esac
}

# `rc=0; verify_one … || rc=$?` — NOT `verify_one … || rc=$?` on one line, and never a trailing
# `[ … ] && worst=…` as the last statement of the loop body: under `set -e` a false test there
# makes the body's exit status non-zero. Both are the shapes that quietly invert a guard.
worst=0
worst_rank=0
for n in "${NAMES[@]}"; do
  rc=0
  verify_one "$n" || rc=$?
  r="$(rank_of "$rc")"
  if [ "$r" -gt "$worst_rank" ]; then worst_rank="$r"; worst="$rc"; fi
  if [ "$ALL" != 1 ]; then break; fi
done
exit "$worst"
